      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Religious institutions foster social trust through the rigorous enforcement of shared norms and moral codes among members, which acts as a powerful informal social control. These institutions also provide robust social capital by creating dense, interpersonally connected networks through participation in rituals and communal activities. The shared belief system offers a collective meaning and purpose that transcends individual concerns, thereby strengthening the group's cohesion and commitment to its stability. This deep, shared covenant often provides a stronger, pre-existing basis for cooperation than purely contract-based secular relationships.",
